import{_ as e,o as t,c as s,Q as a}from"./chunks/framework.b67c47e6.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"Subassemblies","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/management/ifcps/subassemblies/index.md","filePath":"guide/management/ifcps/subassemblies/index.md","lastUpdated":1790931625000}'),n={name:"guide/management/ifcps/subassemblies/index.md"},i=a('<h1 id="subassemblies" tabindex="-1">Subassemblies <a class="header-anchor" href="#subassemblies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Subassemblies&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A <strong>subassembly</strong> is a group of parts inside a panel that your model marks as a unit of its own: a window frame, a lintel or a service box that is built separately and then fitted into the panel. produuz.it reads these groups, gives each one a design of its own next to its panel, and gives every unit of the panel a unit of each of its subassemblies.</p><nav class="table-of-contents"><ul><li><a href="#setting-it-up">Setting it up</a></li><li><a href="#what-the-analysis-does">What the analysis does</a><ul><li><a href="#in-the-designs-table">In the Designs table</a></li></ul></li><li><a href="#parts-units-and-quantities">Parts, units and quantities</a></li><li><a href="#in-the-viewer">In the viewer</a></li><li><a href="#the-checklist">The checklist</a></li></ul></nav><h2 id="setting-it-up" tabindex="-1">Setting it up <a class="header-anchor" href="#setting-it-up" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Setting it up&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open the parsing setup in <strong>Projects Settings → IFC Parsing Setups</strong>, tab <strong>Hierarchy and project</strong>, section <a href="/guide/management/ifcps/#levels-detection">Levels detection</a>, row <strong>Subassembly</strong>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Detection system</th><th>Value</th><th>The subassembly of a part is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>By part property</em></td><td>The name of a property, for example <code>SUBASSEMBLY</code></td><td>The value of that property on the part</td></tr><tr><td><em>By IFC class</em></td><td>Optional: the class of the group, for example <code>IfcElementAssembly</code></td><td>The group the part sits in, when that group is inside the panel: its tag, or its name when it has no tag</td></tr><tr><td><em>By assembly property</em></td><td>The name of a property, for example <code>SUBASSEMBLY</code></td><td>The value of that property on the group the part sits in, when that group is inside the panel</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A part belongs to the <strong>panel</strong> when it has no subassembly, or when its subassembly is the panel&#39;s own name. With <em>By IFC class</em> and <em>By assembly property</em>, a part that sits straight in the panel always belongs to the panel.</p><ul><li>The property name is not case sensitive, and it is looked up in every property set.</li><li>With <em>By IFC class</em>, splitting a project file does not cut a subassembly out as a panel of its own: it stays in its panel&#39;s file.</li></ul><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Most setups already have it</p><p>Most parsing setups have <em>By part property</em> with <code>SUBASSEMBLY</code>. Nothing happens as long as no part in your files carries a property with that name.</p></div><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">When your model nests the subassemblies</p><p>If your exporter writes each subassembly as a group inside the panel, for example an <code>IfcElementAssembly</code> inside the panel&#39;s <code>IfcElementAssembly</code> in Tekla, choose <em>By IFC class</em>. Every group inside the panel then becomes a subassembly, including a group of a single part, as long as the setup keeps that part. Check the panel&#39;s analysis summary after the first analysis.</p></div><h2 id="what-the-analysis-does" tabindex="-1">What the analysis does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-analysis-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the analysis does&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When a panel is analysed, produuz.it collects the subassembly names found on its parts. For each name it:</p><ol><li>Uses the design of the project that has that name, or creates one.</li><li>Links it to the panel and marks it as a subassembly.</li><li>Analyses it again, from the panel&#39;s model file. This happens on every analysis of the panel, so a subassembly always follows its panel&#39;s current model.</li></ol><p>A subassembly has <strong>no model file of its own</strong>. Its analysis, its 3D view and its parts all come from its panel&#39;s latest model file.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">A panel is never turned into a subassembly</p><p>If the project already has a design with that name and its own model file, it is a panel of its own and stays one. The panel&#39;s analysis summary lists the names that could not become subassemblies for that reason. A design with that name and no model file, for example one made from a drawing named after the subassembly, does become the subassembly.</p></div><p>When a name is no longer in the panel&#39;s model, the subassembly is unlinked from the panel. Its design stays in the project: delete it if you do not need it.</p><h3 id="in-the-designs-table" tabindex="-1">In the Designs table <a class="header-anchor" href="#in-the-designs-table" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;In the Designs table&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>A <strong>tree chip</strong> with the number of panels the subassembly belongs to. It is orange when it belongs to none.</li><li>The <strong>IFC</strong> column shows a blue check: the design takes its panel&#39;s model. An orange cross means its panel has no model, or it has no panel.</li><li>The <strong>analysis</strong> column shows a blue check, <em>Analysis done with parent IFC</em>.</li><li>The analysis summary in the panel&#39;s details lists its subassemblies.</li><li>The type of a subassembly cannot be changed by hand: the panel&#39;s analysis sets it.</li></ul><h2 id="parts-units-and-quantities" tabindex="-1">Parts, units and quantities <a class="header-anchor" href="#parts-units-and-quantities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Parts, units and quantities&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The parts of a subassembly are <strong>not</strong> in its panel. They are left out of the panel&#39;s parts, its reports and its machine files, and counted on the subassembly&#39;s units instead. A part is never counted in both.</li><li>Every unit of the panel comes with <strong>one unit of each of its subassemblies</strong>, in the same batch and next to the panel. They are created with the panel&#39;s units, move to another batch with them, and are deleted with them.</li><li>A panel unit that is already started or finished gets no new subassembly units, for example when a subassembly appears in a new model of the panel. It was built from the parts it had.</li><li>If you already added units of a subassembly by hand, the panel&#39;s units take them over, in the order of the batch, instead of making new ones. A unit you added in another batch is left alone.</li><li>Subassemblies do not appear in <a href="/guide/quantities/">Quantities</a>: you plan the panels, and their subassembly units follow. Quantities saved for a design before it became a subassembly are listed in the Generate dialog as <em>now a subassembly</em> and discarded when you generate.</li><li>A subassembly unit has its own status, its own checklist and its own place in the parts list and in logistics.</li></ul><h2 id="in-the-viewer" tabindex="-1">In the viewer <a class="header-anchor" href="#in-the-viewer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;In the viewer&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>A subassembly opens its <strong>panel&#39;s 3D model</strong>. When several panels share the subassembly, a unit opens the model of the panel it belongs to.</li><li>The zone buttons draw the parts of the subassembly. The <strong>PARENT</strong> button draws the rest of the panel around it.</li><li>Drawings, details and documents are the subassembly&#39;s own: the files named after it.</li><li>The <a href="/guide/viewer/#unknown-parts">Unknown Parts</a> buttons list only the subassembly&#39;s own parts. For a panel they leave out the parts of its subassemblies, which are on their own units.</li></ul><p>See <a href="/guide/viewer/">3D Viewer</a> for the rest of the viewer.</p><h2 id="the-checklist" tabindex="-1">The checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A subassembly unit gets the checklist of the production line of its batch, as any panel. Stations and tasks that depend on a zone or a branch look at the <strong>whole panel</strong>, not only at the subassembly, and a panel&#39;s look at its subassemblies too. Keep that in mind when you give a station or a task such a rule.</p>',25),o=[i];function l(r,h,d,p,u,m){return t(),s("div",null,o)}const y=e(n,[["render",l]]);export{c as __pageData,y as default};
